# Docker / Kubernetes / Terraform validation

This pack ships no separate `test` skill (see `pack.json`'s `notes.test`):
validating these file types is a static, tool-driven check, not a written
test suite, and it is exercised from `docker-k8s-terraform-build-fix`'s own
reproduce-and-classify step. This rule states what "validated" means for
each file type so that step — and any manual check — has a concrete bar.

## Dockerfile and Compose

- `hadolint <Dockerfile>` when the project has hadolint configured (a
  `.hadolint.yaml` present, or the tool available on PATH) — catches
  missing pins, `apt-get` without `--no-install-recommends`, and other
  Dockerfile-specific lint findings before a build is even attempted.
- `docker build .` (or `docker compose build` for a multi-service project)
  must exit 0 with no `--no-cache` needed to reproduce a passing state —
  a build that only succeeds from a stale cache is not actually validated.
- For a compose project, `docker compose config` validates the merged
  Compose file parses and every referenced `.env` variable resolves,
  before attempting `up`.

## Kubernetes and Helm

- `kubeconform -strict -summary <manifest-or-dir>` (or `kubeval`, whichever
  the project already has configured) validates every manifest against the
  Kubernetes API schema for the cluster's target version — a manifest that
  parses as YAML can still fail this (unknown field, wrong type) and
  should not be treated as validated on YAML-parse success alone.
- `helm template <chart> | kubeconform -strict` for a Helm chart — template
  rendering can itself fail (a missing `values.yaml` key with no default)
  independently of the rendered manifests' own schema validity, so both
  failure modes need to be distinguished when reporting a finding.
- `kubectl apply --dry-run=server -f <manifest>` (or `--dry-run=client` when
  no live cluster is reachable) catches a resource the API server would
  reject that schema validation alone does not (an immutable field on an
  existing resource, a missing CRD).

## Terraform

- `terraform fmt -check` and `terraform validate` are the syntax/internal-
  consistency floor — both must pass before a `terraform plan` is
  attempted, since `validate` catches type errors and missing required
  arguments that `plan` would otherwise fail on less legibly.
- `terraform plan` (never `apply` from this pack's skills) shows the actual
  proposed change; a plan showing an unexpected destroy/replace on a
  resource the change was not meant to touch is a validation failure in
  its own right, not just a formality to skip past.
- A policy check (`conftest`/OPA, or the project's own policy tool) when
  one is configured — treat its findings the same as a schema-validator
  finding, not as optional advice to skip under time pressure.
